  "explanation": [
    "Verdict: TAMPERED \u2014 7 of 15 checks found strong anomalies (tamper probability 84%, confidence 90%). Critically, 4 independent forensic categories (audio, codec, metadata, quality) detected anomalies simultaneously, which significantly reduces the likelihood of false positive.",
    "[metadata_codec_consistency] score=82%, confidence=85%\n  Finding: audio/video duration mismatch\n  Method: Compares container-level metadata (durations, bitrates, format tags) against the actual stream data for inconsistencies.\n  If tampered: Metadata mismatches can indicate that content was re-muxed, trimmed, or spliced without properly updating container headers.\n  Benign alternative: Legitimate re-encoding, format conversion, or streaming software (OBS, Zoom, etc.) can cause minor metadata drift. Transcoding tools commonly leave software markers.\n  Note: No corroborating signal from other metadata checks.",
    "[qp_consistency] score=100%, confidence=55%\n  Finding: GOP type pattern inconsistency (2/3 non-dominant)\n  Method: Analyzes the pattern of frame types (I/P/B sequences) within each GOP for consistency. Different encoders produce different GOP patterns.\n  If tampered: When segments encoded by different software or settings are joined, the GOP type patterns in the spliced section will differ from the rest of the video.\n  Benign alternative: Scene-based encoding decisions, rate control adjustments, and some streaming protocols intentionally vary GOP patterns for optimal quality.\n  Corroborated by: compression_consistency, double_compression_detection (same forensic category)",
    "[audio_spectral_continuity] score=85%, confidence=56%\n  Finding: audio energy discontinuity at 0.00s; audio spectral_centroid discontinuity at 0.00s; audio spectral_centroid discontinuity at 3.80s (+5 more)\n  Method: Computes audio spectral features (energy, spectral centroid, zero-crossing rate) over sliding windows and detects abrupt discontinuities.\n  If tampered: Audio splices produce sharp spectral breaks because the two joined segments were recorded with different microphones, environments, or encoding settings.\n  Benign alternative: Sudden sounds (door slams, speech starting, music transitions), background noise changes, and microphone handling all create legitimate spectral discontinuities.\n  Note: No corroborating signal from other audio checks.",
    "[double_compression_detection] score=57%, confidence=74%\n  Finding: Periodic pattern at lag 138 suggests prior encoding with different GOP (current GOP=51).\n  Method: Analyzes I-frame size periodicity and P-frame autocorrelation to detect evidence of re-encoding over a previously compressed video.\n  If tampered: When a video is re-encoded, the original GOP structure leaves a periodic fingerprint in frame sizes. A detected period that doesn't match the current GOP strongly indicates re-processing.\n  Benign alternative: Social media uploads, messaging app compression, and cloud storage processing routinely re-encode video. A single re-encoding without content changes is common and not evidence of tampering.\n  Corroborated by: compression_consistency, qp_consistency (same forensic category)",
    "[temporal_noise_consistency] score=85%, confidence=46%\n  Finding: noise_floor shift at 0.5s (z=13.5); noise_floor shift at 1.6s (z=8.8); noise_floor shift at 2.1s (z=26.4)\n  Method: Measures per-frame noise levels (Laplacian noise floor, high-frequency energy) across the timeline to detect source changes.\n  If tampered: Content from a different camera or different encoding quality produces a measurably different noise profile, creating a visible shift at splice boundaries.\n  Benign alternative: Lighting changes (indoor/outdoor transitions, turning lights on), camera gain adjustments (auto-ISO), and scene complexity changes naturally alter noise characteristics.\n  Corroborated by: ela_frame_analysis (same forensic category)",
    "[ela_frame_analysis] score=81%, confidence=41%\n  Finding: ELA ela_mean shift at 0.7s (z=6.3); ELA ela_mean shift at 1.3s (z=4.1); ELA ela_mean shift at 12.6s (z=12.1)\n  Method: Re-compresses sampled frames at a fixed JPEG quality and measures the residual difference. Regions with different compression histories show different error levels.\n  If tampered: Tampered regions that were re-compressed at different quality levels produce ELA residuals that stand out from the surrounding authentic content.\n  Benign alternative: Complex textures, sharp edges, and areas with fine detail naturally produce higher ELA residuals. Scene changes cause legitimate ELA variation across time.\n  Corroborated by: temporal_noise_consistency (same forensic category)",
    "[compression_consistency] score=55%, confidence=52%\n  Finding: P-frame size shift in window 0; P-frame size shift in window 1; P-frame size shift in window 2\n  Method: Splits the video timeline into segments and compares packet-size distributions for each frame type (I/P/B) across those segments.\n  If tampered: If part of the video was re-encoded at different quality settings, that segment will show a measurably different packet-size distribution.\n  Benign alternative: Complex scenes naturally require larger packets than simple ones. Variable bitrate (VBR) encoding, which is the default for most encoders, produces legitimate variation.\n  Corroborated by: double_compression_detection, qp_consistency (same forensic category)",
